<Base
  title="About — Macadish"
  description="Why I measure dough, what a home kitchen can and cannot measure, and the tradeoff most pizza decisions come down to."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="label">Draft · Singapore</p>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I make pizza at home in Singapore, four different doughs, and I write down
      what happens. This is why I bother.
    </p>
  </div>

  <article class="essay">
    <p class="standfirst">
      Most pizza advice is one person's kitchen turned into a rule. I want to know
      which parts actually travel, and which ones only ever worked for them.
    </p>

    <h2>Everyone gets there a different way</h2>

    <p>
      Cold ferment, long ferment, same-day. Bulk before balling, or balling after
      the cold. Poolish, biga, direct. One flour or a blend. 62% hydration or 90%.
      People get to a light, open crust from all of these, and most of them will
      tell you their way is the one that matters.
    </p>

    <p>
      They can't all be right. But they're not all wrong either, and that's the
      interesting part. My guess is that the variables doing the real work aren't
      the ones being argued about. They're the ones nobody can measure at home.
    </p>

    <h2>What I can and can't measure</h2>

    <p>
      Volume doubling. The windowpane. A pH strip. Whether the poolish smells sweet
      or alcoholic. These all work, and I use them. But they're rough measures, meant
      to help you hit the same result twice with your own dough. They aren't precise
      enough to compare two doughs built from different inputs, which is exactly what
      I want to do.
    </p>

    <p>
      What I'd really want is one probe reading pH and acid composition, temperature,
      yeast activity and population, remaining sugar, volatile compounds and rheology.
      Give bakers that and I think a lot of confident advice would quietly go away.
    </p>

    <h2>The tradeoff I keep running into</h2>

    <p>
      Extensible enough to open, strong enough to spring. Almost every choice I make
      is picking a spot between those two. Poolish, warmth and more water buy
      extensibility. Biga, cooler handling and stronger flour buy structure and a
      puffier rim.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once I started thinking about it that way, most decisions got easier. Usually
      I'm not choosing a method. I'm choosing where to sit on that line.
    </p>

    <h2>Fermenting at 29 °C</h2>

    <p>
      My kitchen sits around 29 °C, which is warmer than most recipes assume and warm
      enough that dough gets away from you. So I ferment in a cooler box with an ice
      pack. It isn't a cold hold, it's a slow warming ramp: down to about 9 °C for an
      hour, then climbing to 20 °C over seven hours, capping below room temperature.
    </p>

    <p>
      No European recipe accounts for that. A good chunk of what I've learned is just
      working out which numbers travel and which ones were local all along.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I write down the failures</h2>

    <p>
      A dough that works tells me I'm inside the window. A dough that fails tells me
      where the edge is, which is more useful and more expensive to find out. So the
      over-proofed ones go in the log too, along with the ones that tore on opening,
      and the experiment that came back inconclusive because I couldn't autolyse
      gently with a planetary mixer.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every recipe here has two halves and they matter equally: a number I can repeat,
      and a call I can only make with my hands in the dough. Anyone giving you one
      without the other has left out the hard part.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <p class="note">
      Draft, pulled together from my working notes. Still to be rewritten properly.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

